WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.500
Hadoop.

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.900
Hadoop is legacy now, yet its ideas are everywhere.

3
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.967
Let's see what it welded together.

4
00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:11.333
Hadoop, from two thousand six, is two ideas in one.

5
00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:12.958
HDFS solved storage.

6
00:00:12.958 --> 00:00:20.577
Big files are split into blocks and replicated across cheap nodes, with a namenode tracking where everything lives.

7
00:00:20.577 --> 00:00:22.300
Lose a disk, lose nothing.

8
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.152
MapReduce solved compute.

9
00:00:24.152 --> 00:00:28.000
Instead of moving data to the code, ship the code to the data.

10
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.133
Here is the shape, counting log lines per station.

11
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:37.333
The map step turns each log line into a station and a one, right where the data sits.

12
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:40.567
The shuffle groups those pairs by key across the cluster.

13
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:43.233
And the reduce step adds up each group.

14
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:45.600
Notice that every stage writes to disk.

15
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.467
That disk writing is the catch.

16
00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:52.667
MapReduce is durable, but brutally slow for anything iterative.

17
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:56.800
Spark's in-memory model displaced it for exactly that reason.

18
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.467
Same map, shuffle, reduce shape, minus the disk writes between stages.

19
00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:04.333
You may still meet these commands.

20
00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:07.467
Put copies a local file into HDFS.

21
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:13.467
The listing shows it stored as one hundred twenty eight megabyte blocks, each replicated three times.

22
00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:17.433
And hadoop jar runs a MapReduce job over the whole folder.

23
00:01:18.633 --> 00:01:20.133
Here is where you will meet it.

24
00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:26.900
A partner keeps a decade of mission logs on an on-prem Hadoop cluster that your RAG system must ingest.

25
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:34.200
You will never write MapReduce, but you must read HDFS paths and reason about block sizes and replication.

26
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.767
And you should answer why Spark replaced Hadoop in one sentence.

27
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:39.852
Let's recap.

28
00:01:39.852 --> 00:01:43.467
HDFS stores replicated blocks across cheap nodes.

29
00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:46.133
MapReduce is map, shuffle, reduce.

30
00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:48.733
Disk writes between stages made it slow.

31
00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:51.826
But its vocabulary lives on everywhere.

32
00:01:51.826 --> 00:01:52.900
Next up: Kafka.
